 ## Background
-The foundational principles of volume compositing were established in the early 1980s with the introduction of the volume rendering integral and front-to-back/back-to-front accumulation algorithms by Williams et al. and Levoy [S1, unofficial][S2]. The specific "Source-Attenuation Reprojection" technique was developed by Vannier et al. for medical data visualization, particularly in stereotactic neurosurgery planning, to handle depth shading and surface definition through opacity thresholding [S1, unofficial]. The "Reconstruction Validity Weighted" approach was introduced by Zhang and Tang (2019) to improve probabilistic classification visualization by incorporating kernel regression validity metrics into the compositing loop [S2]. The "Final Composition" method involving diffusion and anisotropic scattering for global illumination in clouds was detailed by Kajiya and others in the context of volumetric cloud rendering, utilizing convolution solutions for low-density segments and diffusion solutions for high-density regions [S3, unofficial]. The "ComposedVolumeStyle" is part of the InstantReality 1.0 framework, providing a node-based approach to combining shading and opacity mapping styles [S4, unofficial].
+The foundational optical model for volume compositing, specifically the front-to-back alpha blending equation, is widely attributed to Porter and Duff's work on image compositing, adapted for volumetric data by Levoy [S12, S43, S50, unofficial]. The specific application of this model to direct volume ray casting allows for the visualization of internal structures without surface extraction [S4, unofficial]. The concept of source-attenuation reprojection was developed by Vannier et al. for medical data visualization, aiming to improve depth shading by tracing rays to surfaces [S1, unofficial]. Reconstruction validity weighted rendering was introduced by Zhang and Tang to address classification artifacts by incorporating kernel regression accuracy into the compositing process [S2]. The integration of pre-integrated textures for final scene composition was pioneered by Krüger and Westermann to accelerate the rendering of participating media over opaque geometry [S1, unofficial]. Differentiable compositing for training neural radiance fields (NeRFs) and other implicit representations has become a standard technique in recent years, enabling end-to-end learning of volumetric representations [S1, unofficial].
 
